For any function $f$ in $\bmrm[1]{L}(\mathbb{R})$ or $\bmrm{L}(\mathbb{R})$, we denote its Fourier transform by $\mathcal{F}_{f}$, i.e.~
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{F}_f(\xi)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{\mathbb{R}} e^{-{\rm{i}}x\xi} f(x)\,dx,\:  \xi\in\mathbb{R}.
\end{equation*}

As before we define $\mathcal{F}^{e}$ the shifted Fourier transform, i.e.~for any $f\in\bmrm{L}(\mathbb{R},e)$, writing $e(x)=e^{x}$,
\begin{align}
\mathcal{F}^{e}_f(\xi)=\mathcal{F}_{\sqrt{e}f} (\xi)
\end{align}
